le/PDP-endian), four bytes (UTF-32, prebuilt), no BOM (prebuilt), the whole\ntext as one BIG integer, or a knob on an older day's codec (code-unit escapes byte-swapped, bit order, alphabet).\nD21 (\"A familiar wrapper. Something compressed inside.\" / \"Under Pressure\") opened a new family, COMPRESSION: GZIP\ninside standard padded BASE64 (`H4sIAAAAAAACA...`, header `1f8b0800 00000000 0203`); Mastery line 21 = each 36-char\nfragment its own gzip member. \"Familiar wrapper\" = an already-seen codec as the OUTER layer. Expect the D19->D20\npattern again (\"same wrapper, other squeeze\") -- and D22 delivered it: \"Another familiar wrapper. This one needs a little\ninflation.\" / \"Deflated Ego\" = ZLIB (`78da`, level 9) inside std padded BASE64 (`eNo..`/`eNp..`/`eNr..`); Mastery line\n22 = each 20-char fragment its own zlib stream. The D21 solver's `_b64z` (magic sniff incl. `\\x78`) passed it unchanged.\nRHYTHM since D19: day N opens a family, day N+1 turns ONE knob (D19 LE -> D20 BE, D21 gzip -> D22 zlib). D23-D25 each\nopened a NEW family instead: D23 \"The alphabet got bigger. Adobe might recognize the neighbors.\" = ASCII85 `<~...~>`\n(Mastery line 23 own delimiters); D24 \"Count carefully. The comma knows where to stop.\" / \"String Theory\" = DJB\nNETSTRINGS `1:N,1:i,...` (one char per frame, payload `,`/`:` framed as `1:,,`/`1::,`; line 24 `1:o,1:n,...`); D25\n\"Every character has a point. A code point, anyway.\" / \"You Have a Point\" = Unicode CODE POINTS `U+004E U+0069 ...`\n(UPPERCASE, 4 hex digits, single spaces, 762 B = 7 B per char; Mastery line 25 = 5 tokens per fragment), one layer,\nplain text inside -- a return to the D7-D9 CHARACTER-NOTATION family (D9 was `\"\\u00XX\"`), predicted here as row 22\n(prebuilt `uplus_dec`, single CORRECT ~27 s). Only a \"Same ...\"/\"Another ...\" prompt has signalled a knob day.\nD26 (\"A very small language has quite a lot to say.\" / \"Food for Thought\" = brain food) opened a NEW family,\nESOTERIC LANGUAGES: a BRAINFUCK program whose output is the sentence -- only `+ - .`, one cell, each char = a run of one\nsign equal to the delta from the previous char, then `.` (3406 B, ~31 B per char; Mastery line 26 = the same over a\n5-char tail, 166-217 B). Row 83 had rated it LOW (\"program, not encoding\"): the pun named a LANGUAGE, not a codec. The\nUnicode/notation queue (rows 173-182, all built) did not come. Mastery broke on it: the solver's runtime DAYS encoder\n(absolute `+`*ord) did not round-trip the GM's delta bytes -> 64.9 s timeout (see mastery-line-routing).\nD27 (\"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems chatty.\" / \"Monkey Business\") was the BF KNOB predicted here, (a) first: OOK!, the\nexact D26 one-cell delta program re-spelled as `Ook.`/`Ook!` word PAIRS (single-spaced, no `Ook?` used, ~314 B per\nchar, 33929 B; Mastery line 27 = 1672-2172 B printing a 4-5 char tail). A knob day WITHOUT a \"Same\"/\"Another\" cue:\nthe prompt quoted the dialect's tokens. Prebuilt `warmups_codecs.bfd_dec` (row 183) covered it; single CORRECT ~33 s,\nTT/Mastery first try (Mastery solve 34.9 s: the solver's own DAYS[27] encoder again was not GM-faithful).\nD28 (\"Our sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards.\" / \"Back in Sixteen\") left the esolangs after two days and\nREMIXED two early days: D3 HEX (the prompt re-uses D3's \"sixteen-fingered friend\" riddle) + D12 REVERSAL (\"walking\nbackwards\"; title = Back in Black) -> the hex string reversed CHARACTER-wise (220 B, `[0-9a-e]`, ends `...6e4`); Mastery\nline 28 = each 4-5 char tail's hex reversed on its own (8/10 chars). Row 104 (prebuilt tgt:free-over-strict) and\n`decode_chunk` (rev + hex) covered it: race probe CORRECT at start+8 s; TT/Mastery (138 x 28, solve 6.2 s) first try.\nNo DAYS[28] was registered -- line 28 survived only as the LAST line (mastery-line-28-revhex).\nD29-D31 forecast, in order: (a) MORE REMIXES of two seen days cued by quoting their old riddles: reversal at another\ngranularity or over another codec (reversed binary/octal/base64/Morse, byte-order vs char-order, row 201), any\nfree transform over any strict string or a strict string of a transformed text (rot13 of base64, Atbash/rail of hex,\nbase64 of ROT47 -- row 202 composition matrix over every DAYS codec); (b) a FINALE recap: one blob mixing several seen\ncodecs in segments (row 203), or an esolang printing a seen codec (row 196); (c) a third esolang day (rows 192-200) is\nnow less likely but stays queued. Rule for every day: a strict decode that SUCCEEDS into junk = an outer free layer.\nSnapshot 2026-10-28 (after D28; new rows 201-203).\n\nStatus legend: `seen Dn` = GM-confirmed day codec (`lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS`, `lib.decode.decode_chunk`/`FREE_CODECS`).\n`prebuilt tgt:<id>` = speculative race module + fixtures from `tests/data/warmups_fixtures/TARGETS.json` (KEPT in\n`.run/prebuild-state.json`). `prebuilt wc:<name>` = encoder/decoder pair in `lib.warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES` (used by\n`infer`/`plaintext`/today-probe; free-text ones also `SPECULATIVE_FREE`). `prebuilt dec:<fn>` = `lib.decode` branch.\n`gap` = nothing in lib (a standard tool may still decode it: chepy 7.x and CyberChef at `tools/npm-cyberchef` are\ninstalled; `.venv` has `base58`, `pycipher`, `Crypto`; NOT installed: base45/base91/pybase62/pybase100/z85/unidecode).\n\n**Crib fact (all keyed rows):** the first 87 plaintext chars are known, so ANY fixed-key letter cipher leaks its key\nfrom the blob alone: Vigenere/Beaufort/Gronsfeld/autokey key = (c - p) / (p - c) / (k - c) over the crib, period =\nsmallest repeat; keyword substitution = the crib map (21 of 26 letters; only f j q y z unseen, slug-only).\n\"Keyless?\" below therefore means \"decodable without outside info\": title pun OR crib.\n\n## Coverage table\n\n| # | concept | status | keyless? | recognition cues | standard decode | D19+ likelihood |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | base64 (std, padded) | seen D1 | yes | `A-Za-z0-9+/`, `=` pad, len%4==0, x1.33 | `base64 -d`; `base64.b64dec