g; Mastery-line sized fragments (8-40 chars) too.\"},\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"970ca733-aece-4114-b789-a48808ed5691","uuid":"a2fb6076-7869-4bb5-a824-6bcbf70a8e2c","timestamp":"2026-10-31T04:37:11.363Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Readiness gaps\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\": \"mastery-line-28-revhex\", \"concept\": \"carried solver/lib: register the day-28 reversed-hex codec for Mastery line 28\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D28 Mastery passed with NO DAYS[28]/register_free for day 28: line 28 was the LAST line, so _positional's unknown-last-line path (decode_chunk: reversal then hex) decoded it. On D29+ line 28 is a MIDDLE line. Add lib DAYS (28, 'revhex', lambda s: s.encode().hex()[::-1], lambda t: bytes.fromhex(t[::-1]).decode(), False) -- GM-faithful (lowercase hex, char-reversed; 138/138 Mastery cd6a5b33 line-28 fragments decode) -- or register_free('revhex', 28, fn) in the carried solver.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"reversal-granularity-over-codec\", \"concept\": \"reversal at another granularity or over another seen codec (byte-order vs char-order hex, reversed binary/octal/decimal/base64/base32/a85/Morse/Bacon/UTF-16 hex, reversed BF/Ook program)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Direct knob of D28 ('sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards' = char-reversed hex). rev_any(t): for each seen strict decoder D in warmups_codecs.DAYS and each reversal R in {chars, tokens (split on the blob's separator), bytes after decode, bits per byte (LSB-first), nibbles}, try D(R(t)) and R(D(t)); also padding/BOM/'<~' at the wrong end as a cue. Fixtures: reversed '8-bit binary' (string and per-byte bit order), reversed octal token order and digit string, reversed base64 ('==' first), reversed base32, reversed Ascii85 ('>~...~<'), reversed Morse, reversed fffe-UTF-16 hex, reversed one-cell BF program. Gate printable + slug; Mastery-line sized fragments (8-40 chars) too.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"composition-matrix-depth2\", \"concept\": \"two seen day codecs composed in either order (rot13 of base64, Atbash/rail of hex, base64 of ROT47, URL-encoded base64, Morse of base32, hex of reversed text)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"D28 opened the 'remix' family (D3 hex + D12 reversal, cued by quoting D3's riddle). compose2(t): enumerate outer in (free transforms: rev, rot13, Atbash, caesar-k, ROT47, rail 2-5) + every DAYS decoder, then the inner via decode_chunk; strict decoders first (cheap fail), free transforms only where the charset refuses or yields junk; time cap ~0.2 s per blob, slug gate. Build the matrix FROM warmups_codecs.DAYS so tomorrow's codec joins automatically. Fixtures: every ordered pair of D1-D28 codecs over the crib sentence (skip pairs that collapse to identity); Mastery-length fragments.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"mixed-segment-recap-blob\", \"concept\": \"finale recap: one single-solve blob made of segments in different seen codecs (unnumbered lines, separators, or a codec per segment)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Last days (D29-D31) may recap the lane: a Mastery set without NN<TAB> numbering, or a sentence split into chunks each in a different day's codec. recap_dec(t): split on newlines, then on charset boundaries ('<~'/'~>', 'U+', '1:', runs of [0-9a-f], runs of '+-.', 'Ook'); decode each chunk with decode_chunk / DAYS decoders, join verbatim (no strip), slug gate; fall back to a DP over split points when no separators. Fixtures: the crib sentence cut into 5-10 chunks in random seen codecs, with and without newlines.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"mastery-bf-line-26\", \"concept\": \"carried solver: Mastery esolang lines (26 BF, 27 Ook) must be routed by a GM-faithful encoder or a decode-only registration\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D26: the solver's runtime DAYS[26] encoder ('+'*ord per cell + '.>') did not reproduce the GM's one-cell signed-delta bytes -> line 26 never round-tripped -> 64.9 s, 0 answers. Lib now has DAYS[26] with bf_enc (delta form). D27 REPEATED it: the solver appended (27, 'ook', _ook_enc, _ook_dec) with the same absolute form spelled in Ook -> line 27 failed on 133/133 sets -> solve 34.9 s of the 60 s budget (passed only because DAYS[26] was fixed). Tomorrow's copied solver must, BEFORE anything else: replace the runtime DAYS[27] append with (27, 'ook', lambda s: warmups_codecs.bfd_enc(s, 'ook'), warmups_codecs.bfd_dec, False) (or register_free('ook', 27, ...)) unless lib's DAYS already has day 27, then hctf solver-test auto-mastery-c6135949 (133 x 27) and check solve < 5 s. Same rule for every future program/dialect day: lib encoder or register_free.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"rhythm-broken-structure-first\", \"concept\": \"when the day is a NEW family with no charset fingerprint, read the blob's structure first\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D24 broke the D19-D23 'new family, then one knob' rhythm (no Ascii85 knob; framing instead), and nothing prebuilt covered it. Worker rule: if STEP 1 (lib) misses, describe the repeating unit (here '\\\\d+:.,' x 111) and name the spec from it + the title pun before brute-forcing codecs; write a strict whole-text parser that returns None on mismatch so it can sit first in the pipeline. D25 confirmed the pattern (third new family in a row: code points, a D9 sibling); the prebuilt row-22 decoder caught it, so keep the queue wide across families. D26 made it four new families in a row and the prompt named a LANGUAGE: a blob with a tiny operator alphabet (`+ - .`) and run structure is a PROGRAM -> interpret it (pure python, step cap), don't table-decode. D27 was a KNOB (Ook!, D26's BF re-spelled) with no 'Same'/'Another' cue: the prompt simply quoted the tokens ('Ook. Ook? Ook!'). A prompt that quotes tokens/words = the blob's alphabet; look the dialect up in lib (bfd_dec) before writing a decoder.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"mastery-line-routing\", \"concept\": \"Mastery: every day's codec must claim its RAW line inside decode_chunked / _joint_free\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D23 Mastery lost its first submit (113/113 sets: right slug words, wrong NN, every digit +5): the solver pre-decoded line 23 ('<~..~>' -> 'el-46') in place, and the joint free-codec search -- which has no identity option -- gave the plain line a digit ROT5. Rule: never rewrite numbered lines before decode_chunked; add the day's codec via register_free(name, day, fn) (gated to its own charset/delimiters) or decode_chunk. Before any timed start, print one set's joined sentence and check the slug digits against the single-solve shape. Same fields wrong on every set = one line's routing. D24 confirmed the rule: _netstr registered as register_free('netstr', 24, ...) and skipped as a whole-blob step for numbered blobs -> Mastery 118 x 24 first try. D25 REPEATED the bug through another path (123/123 sets NN wrong): _uplus_lines pre-decoded line 25 and, with no day-25 codec registered, decode._positional sent the plain line to _unknown_line_cands, which re-decoded it. Checklist for every new day: (1) register_free(name, day, fn) for TODAY's day number; (2) the whole-blob step must skip numbered Mastery blobs; (3) print one set's joined sentence and compare NN with the raw last line before submitting. D26 broke it a THIRD way: the solver appended a runtime warmups_codecs.DAYS entry (26, 'brainfuck', enc='+'*ord(c)+'.>', _bf) whose encoder does not reproduce the GM's one-cell DELTA bytes; _positional's re-encode check rejected line 26 on all 128 sets -> _unknown_line_cands + joint search per set -> solve 64.9 s, 0 answers (harness kill); a live worker hand-decoded and submitted. (4) A DAYS entry must round-trip a REAL Mastery line (assert enc(dec(line)) == line) -- many-to-one codecs (programs, whitespace/padding variants) go through register_free instead; (5) time hctf solver-test on a real Mastery case before the timed start.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"byte-family-mastery-cost\", \"concept\": \"Mastery solve time per set as codecs accumulate; lane-solver continuity\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D21 Mastery (103 x 21) died at the 60 s harness budget because solve.py was an ad-hoc generic per-line brute force, not the carried D20 lane solver; the D20 solver + one `_b64z` branch did 0.34 s. D22 Mastery (108 x 22) passed first try with the D21 solver unchanged (plus the solverrt partial-emit wrapper) but solve took 15.9 s and submit 11.8 s -- profile per set if it grows; D23 (113 x 23) solved in 1.2 s. Rule: copy the previous day's solve.py, add today's codec as one whole-blob step + register_free(name, day, fn), return early, time hctf solver-test on the real Mastery case before any timed start. D24 (118 x 24): solve 1.6 s, whole Mastery 4.5 s. D25 (123 x 25): solve 0.76 s. D28 138 x 28: solve 6.2 s (D27 34.9 s was the bad Ook encoder, not set count).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"program-output-redecode\", \"concept\": \"esolang output that is itself encoded (BF/Ook printing base64/hex/rot13/Morse/decimal codes) and programs under a seen wrapper (base64/hex/gzip of BF or Ook)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Likely stack after two esolang days (the lane stacked twice before: D16-D17, D19-D22). Make every interpreter (bf_dec, bfd_dec, deadfish, whitespace, befunge, chef, vm) feed its output back through decode.decode_chunk / peel when it is printable but not crib/slug-shaped (bounded depth 3), and make peel try every interpreter on wrapper output that matches an esolang charset ('+-<>[].,' soup, Ook tokens, 'Moo', i/d/s/o). Register as a whole-blob step and as a free codec for Mastery fragments. Fixtures: BF printing base64 of the sentence, Ook printing hex, BF printing rot13, base64 of Ook, gzip+base64 of BF, hex of BF; decoys: BF printing plain English (must not be re-decoded into garbage -- keep the plain reading when it already ends in a slug).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"bf-full-programs\", \"concept\": \"full Brainfuck: loops/multiplication generators, many cells, comment text between commands, wrap below 0, ',' input, 16-bit cells, BF under a seen wrapper\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Knob on D26 (only + - . seen). Move the D26 solver's _bf into lib.warmups_codecs.bf_run(prog, steps=10**7, cells=65536, bits=8) + bf_dec(t): accept when, after removing non-command chars, the program has balanced brackets and >= 1 '.', run it, printable + slug gate; try 8-bit wrap first, then 16-bit/unbounded cells if output is junk; ',' reads 0 (or EOF=-1 variant). Also peel: if a base64/hex/gzip decode yields BF soup, run it (stack with decompress-then-redecode). Encoders for fixtures: D26 delta style, multiplication-loop generator (n = a*b + c per char), cell-per-char with '>' and a shared 10x loop (the common 'bf text generator' output). Decoys: Morse lines (. - space) must not be claimed unless the program output passes the slug gate.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"deadfish\", \"concept\": \"Deadfish (i d s o; value reset to 0 at 256 or -1) and the XKCD variant (x k c d)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'A very small language' read literally (4 commands, output = the number, or chr(number) in the char variant). deadfish_dec(t): only [idso] (or [xkcd]) + whitespace; v=0; i +1, d -1, s v*v, o -> chr(v) (char-output variant, what a text-printing challenge needs; also collect numbers and try chr); after each op, v in {-1, 256} -> 0. Also accept uppercase. Fixtures: delta style (i/d runs then o) and square-based style (s to jump), XKCD letters; decoys: English words made of i/d/s/o only ('dido') must fail the slug gate.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"whitespace-language\", \"concept\": \"Whitespace programming language (space/tab/LF; also visible S/T/L or [Space][Tab][LF] notation)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Esolang sibling of D26. ws_dec(t): keep only ' ', '\\\\t', '\\\\n' (or map S/T/L letters, [Space]/[Tab]/[LF] tokens); parse IMPs: SS push (sign S=+ T=-, binary S=0 T=1, L end), SLS dup, SLT swap, SLL drop, TSSS add, TSST sub, TSSL mul, TLSS output char, TLST output num, LSS label, LSL call, LLL end, etc.; run with a step cap; join output. Must sit BEFORE whitespace-stego (row 106) and zero-width decoders, and those must stay as fallbacks. Fixtures: push+print per char (typical generator output), with labels/loops, visible S/T/L notation; decoys: whitespace stego blobs (8-bit space/tab).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"unseparated-token-bf\", \"concept\": \"BF token dialects with UNKNOWN or UNSEPARATED tokens: new word triads paired like Ook (Meep. Meep? Meep!), emoji BF, CamelCase/no-space words, multi-char glyph units\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Direct knob of D27 (same program, other spelling). bfd_dec's crib solver assumes separable tokens; add a segmenter: grapheme clusters (emoji with ZWJ/VS16 kept whole), [A-Z][a-z]* runs, word+[.?!] marks, fixed-width k-char units (k=2..8) when the text has no separators. If <= 3 distinct units, try Ook-style PAIRING (9 ordered pairs -> 8 commands; pair from offset 0 and 1). Then assign units/pairs to +-<>[].,: the GM's delta program has exactly three used commands with run structure (+ runs, - runs, single '.' between runs) -> the unit that never repeats twice in a row is '.', the two run units are +/- (decide by the crib: first output must be 'N' = 78 from cell 0). Run bf_dec, slug gate. Fixtures: Meep/Hmm/Eek triads, emoji 3-unit and 8-unit maps without spaces, CamelCase 'MooMooMoo', 2-char units 'aZaZbY'; decoys: Morse, plain prose, base64.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"brain-flak\", \"concept\": \"Brain-Flak ((), [], {}, <> only; two stacks) printing the sentence\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'Brain' sibling of BF/Ook. brainflak_dec(t): whole text in '()[]{}<>' + whitespace, balanced; nilads () = 1, [] = stack height, {} = pop, <> = toggle stacks; monads (X) = push value of X (and evaluate to it), [X] = negative of X, {X} = loop while top != 0, <X> = evaluate X, return 0. Output = final active stack from TOP to bottom as chr (the -A ASCII flag); also try bottom-to-top and decimal numbers. Step cap 10^7. Fixtures: the standard 'push each char as ((()()()...){}...)' generator (repeated doubling), a loop-based generator; decoys: balanced bracket soup from JSON/Lisp (must fail the slug gate).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"chicken-animal-esolangs\", \"concept\": \"animal-word esolangs: Chicken (opcode = number of 'chicken' words per line), Hodor, Meow-likes, other one-word languages\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'Monkey Business' -> another animal day is a plausible pun path (COW and Pikalang are built in bfd_dec). Chicken VM: stack[0] = pointer to stack, stack[1] = user input, program = list of line word-counts; ops 0 axe(exit) 1 chicken(push 'chicken') 2 add 3 fox(sub) 4 rooster(mul) 5 compare 6 pick(load; next op is the source flag) 7 peck(store) 8 fr(jump if) 9 BBQ(top -> '&#N;' HTML entity char) 10+ push n-10; output = top of stack at exit (often a string of &#N; entities -> html.unescape). Hodor: Hodor/hodor/HODOR/Hodor? variants map to BF (treat as row-194 unknown map). Fixtures: Chicken program printing the sentence via BBQ, a 'chicken' line count generator; decoys: prose with repeated words.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"spoon-prefix-code-bf\", \"concept\": \"Spoon / prefix-code BF (1=+ 000=- 010=> 011=< 00100=[ 0011=] 001010=. 0010110=,) and BF packed as bits in hex/base64\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Binary spelling of BF without fixed group width (Binaryfuck 3-bit is built). spoon_dec(t): strip whitespace, require [01]+, greedy prefix-code parse (also the inverted polarity and the 00101110 debug / 00101111 exit codes), then bf_dec. Also unpack hex/base64 bytes to bits MSB- and LSB-first and try the same. Fixtures: Spoon of the delta program, packed into bytes then hex/base64; decoys: 8-bit binary ASCII (D4 must still win via slug gate order).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"boolfuck-bitlevel-bf\", \"concept\": \"bit-level BF: Boolfuck (+ flips bit, ; outputs bit, bytes LSB-first), Smallfuck, Bitfuck\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"boolfuck_dec(t): alphabet '+,;<>[]' (no '-' or '.'); bit tape, '+' flips, ';' appends the current bit to the output bit stream, ',' reads 0; bytes assembled LSB-first (also try MSB-first) -> printable + slug gate; step cap. Smallfuck ('*' flips, no output: skip). Fixtures: the standard Boolfuck text generator output for the sentence; decoys: BF programs (no ';').\"},\n  {\"id\": \"print-statement-esolangs\", \"concept\": \"print-statement / prose languages: LOLCODE (VISIBLE), ArnoldC (TALK TO THE HAND), INTERCAL (PLEASE READ OUT, Turing-Text-Model), Rockstar (poetic literals, Say), Emojicode\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'Chatty'/'say' re-read. Extract string literals from keyword frames (VISIBLE \\\"..\\\", TALK TO THE HAND \\\"..\\\", Say \\\"..\\\", 🗨 / 😀 🔤..🔤) and join; LOLCODE ':)' newline escapes. INTERCAL: ',1 SUB #n <- #v' arrays + READ OUT ,1 -> Turing Text Model decode (each value v: prev_bits - v mod 256, then reverse the 8 bits of the result to get the char). Rockstar poetic number literals: each word's length mod 10 = a digit ('.' = decimal point) -> numbers -> chr, 'Say/Shout/Whisper X'. Fixtures each printing the sentence; decoys: plain English with 'say'.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"befunge-2d\", \"concept\": \"Befunge-93/98 and ><> (2-D grid languages)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"befunge_dec(t): grid 80x25 (or text bounds), PC starts (0,0) moving right; implement 0-9 + - * / % ! ` > < ^ v ? (deterministic: treat ? as >) _ | \\\" : \\\\\\\\ $ . , # g p & ~ @ ; string mode pushes ords; ',' outputs chr; step cap 10^6. ><>: o outputs, ; ends, ' and \\\" strings, mirrors / \\\\\\\\ | _ #. Fixtures: the common '\\\"!dlrow olleH\\\">:#,_@' style generator for the sentence, multi-line v/< loops, ><> version; decoys: ASCII art (row 181).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"jsfuck-aaencode\", \"concept\": \"JSFuck ([]()!+ only), aaencode (Japanese emoticons), jjencode ($=~[] style) -- JS-from-tiny-alphabet encodings\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"NEVER node/eval (untrusted program). JSFuck: a pure-python evaluator for the JSFuck expression algebra (values: [], ![], +[], []+[], string indexing, [][\\\"flat\\\"] -> function source text constants, Function('return ...')() for the final string; implement the ~40 primitives the jsfuck.com encoder uses) and read the string passed to the final call (eval/Function body) -- usually alert(\\\"...\\\") or the bare string. aaencode/jjencode: known static decoders (regex the per-char octal/hex digit expressions, e.g. aadecode by replacing the emoticon digit symbols) -> the JS source -> extract the string literal. Fixtures from a vendored encoder (pure python port) for the sentence; decoys: base64/ROT47 soups.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"chef-recipe-lang\", \"concept\": \"Chef (recipe-shaped language) and Shakespeare (play-shaped) programs that print text\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'Food for Thought' -> 'recipe' pun risk. chef_dec(t): parse 'Ingredients.' (amount [measure] name; amounts = char codes), 'Method.' steps: Put X into [the] [nth] mixing bowl (push), Fold, Add/Remove/Combine/Divide, Liquefy [contents of the] mixing bowl (mark as chars), Pour contents of the [nth] mixing bowl into the [nth] baking dish, 'Serves N.' (output dishes, top of stack first = REVERSED push order); loops 'Verb the ingredient.' ... 'Verb until verbed.' with a step cap. Shakespeare: subset (characters, 'You are as <adj> as <noun>' arithmetic = powers of 2, 'Speak your mind!' outputs chr). Fixtures: the common 'Hello World Souffle' style generator for the sentence; decoys: plain English recipes without Method.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"stack-vm-listing\", \"concept\": \"toy stack-VM / assembly / one-liner listings that print characters (PUSH 78 / OUT, Forth 78 emit, dc 78P, PostScript, mov al,0x4e; HQ9+)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'A very small language' read as a tiny instruction set. vm_dec(t): tokenise lines into mnemonic + int args (decimal/hex/char literal); simulate a generic machine: PUSH/LOAD/MOV/SET n, ADD/SUB/INC/DEC/MUL, OUT/PRINT/PUTC/EMIT/WRITE/P/INT 0x10 (print low byte or top of stack), JMP/LOOP only with a step cap; Forth 'n emit', dc 'nP' / 'n P', PostScript '(..) show'. Output gate: printable + slug. NEVER execute as real code. Fixtures: 4-5 synthetic dialects (stack, register, Forth, dc, 8086-ish) printing the sentence; decoys: decimal char-code lists (D5 must still win).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"codepoint-offset-block\", \"concept\": \"code-point arithmetic: constant offset K over every code point (Caesar on code points), text shifted into another Unicode block, XOR mask on code points\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Crib makes it keyless: K = ord(t[0]) - ord('N') (or from the first U+ token), verify K on the whole 87-char crib. Cover: generic +K (incl. K < 0 and K moving ASCII into CJK/PUA/astral), fullwidth (+0xFEE0, space -> U+3000), Tags (+0xE0000), enclosed/squared/negative-squared Latin (letter base per case, digits separate), math alphanumerics (per-style base table; NFKC handles most), XOR K on code points. Fixtures per block + U+ token form of a shifted text + decoys (ROT8000 must still route to rot8000).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"unicode-tag-smuggling\", \"concept\": \"invisible Unicode smuggling: Tags block (U+E0020-E007E) ASCII smuggling and variation-selector byte smuggling (U+FE00-FE0F, U+E0100-E01EF) after a carrier emoji\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). AI-arena-topical 'invisible instruction' encodings. tags_dec: keep chars in U+E0000-E007F -> chr(cp-0xE0000) (drop U+E0001 language tag / U+E007F cancel). vs_dec: VS1-16 -> 0-15, VS17-256 -> 16-255, bytes -> UTF-8 (Paul Butler emoji smuggling); ignore the visible carrier chars. Must read the artifact as UTF-8 (astral). Fixtures: tags after visible text, tags alone, emoji+VS, mixed with zero-width (zero-width decoder must not steal), decoys (real emoji sequences with VS16 U+FE0F only).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"mixed-escape-soup\", \"concept\": \"every character in a randomly chosen notation (U+004E, \\\\u0069, &#99;, &#x65;, %20, \\\\x77, 0x6f, \\\\162, named entities)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Likely Time Trial / Mastery MUTATION of the D7/D8/D9/D25 codecs ('new unseen effects'). One alternation tokenizer over URL %xx, %uXXXX, HTML &#N; &#xH; &name;, \\\\uXXXX, \\\\UXXXXXXXX, \\\\u{..}, \\\\xNN, \\\\NNN octal, U+XXXX, 0xNN; whole text must tokenise (allow single separators); per-token radix. Prefer stdlib (html.unescape, unquote) only after splitting. Fixtures: uniform random mixtures, per-word notation, with and without separators; decoys: single-notation blobs (must give the same text).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"netstring-knobs\", \"concept\": \"netstring knobs: len > 1 and mixed lengths, len in UTF-8 bytes vs characters, leading zeros, whitespace/newlines between frames, missing final comma, nested netstrings, frames whose payload is a seen codec\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Most likely D25 knob on D24 'String Theory' (1-char netstrings). Generalise warmups_codecs.netstr_dec into netstr_any(t): cursor over BYTES (t.encode('utf-8')) and over chars, both tried; allow optional whitespace/newline between frames and a missing final ','; leading zeros in len; if every payload is itself a netstring run, recurse; if payloads are not English, run decode_chunk on each payload AND on the joined payload (frames of base64/hex/rot13). Whole text must parse or return None. Register in CANDIDATES + decode_chunk + as a free codec for 8-40 char Mastery fragments. Fixtures: len 1 / 2-9 / random mixed, UTF-8 multibyte payload (byte vs char), nested, b64-per-frame, decoys (plain prose with '3:' times).\"},\n  {\"id\": \"indexed-fragments-shuffled\", \"concept\": \"index-tagged frames out of order (`17:a,3:b`, `a@17`, `[17]a`) and run-count frames (`3:o,` = 'ooo')\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). 'Count carefully' re-read: the number before ':' may be a POSITION (frames shuffled; sort by index, check 0..n-1 or 1..n with no gaps/repeats) or a REPEAT COUNT (RLE: payload char x N). netstring len and count read the same when all N=1, so decide by N > 1 with a 1-char payload (count) or by a permutation of range(n) (index). Build frames_dec(t) trying netstring -> index-sort -> run-count, slug-gated. Fixtures: shuffled index frames (0- and 1-based, separators , ; space), RLE colon form 'N:c,' and 'c*N', decoys.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"tnetstrings\", \"concept\": \"tnetstrings (typed netstrings): `<len>:<data><tag>` with tags , string # int ^ float ! bool ~ null ] list } dict\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Sibling spec of D24 (Zed Shaw's typed netstrings). Recursive cursor parser: len, ':', len chars, one tag char; ']' / '}' payloads parse recursively; collect string leaves in order (and dict values) and join; slug-gate. Cue: netstring shape whose terminators vary over ',#^!~]}'. Fixtures: flat strings, a list of 1-char strings, a dict {i: char}, nested.\"},\n  {\"id\": \"bencode-sexp\", \"concept\": \"bencode (`4:spam`, `i42e`, `l..e`, `d..e`) and Rivest canonical/transport S-expressions (`(3:abc5:hello)`, `[3:mime]`, `{base64}`, `|b64|`, `#hex#`)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"BUILT (prebuild ok). Same 'len:data' atom as netstrings but NO trailing comma (BitTorrent bencode; SPKI canonical sexp). Cursor parser: digits ':' -> string of len; 'i'..'e' int; 'l'